  Cheap to change later: adding call sites, adding the session ID. Nothing here is expensive to reverse — this is the
  least locked-in decision of the four.

  [1mMy recommendation: existing call sites + global error capture + a session ID on every record.[0m The global handlers are
  the actual answer to "debug production issues," and the session ID costs about three lines while making a console dump
  from a user readable as a sequence rather than loose lines. I'd skip the timing instrumentation — there's nothing
  slow here yet to measure.

[38;5;246m⏺ [39mUser answered Claude's questions:
[38;5;246m  ⎿  · How much should the logger instrument? → Call sites + global errors + session ID (Recommended)[39m

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Last question before I present the design — [1mtooling.[0m [38;5;153mpackage.json[39m has no dependencies, no scripts, no test runner, and
  no linter. Cheapest moment to add any of that is now, before the code exists.

  The logger has logic genuinely worth testing: the recursive redaction walk and level filtering are where bugs hide,
  and a redaction bug is the one failure here with real consequences. Note this doesn't force the ESM conversion I ruled
  out — [38;5;153mlogger.js[39m can end with a three-line dual-export footer ([38;5;153mif (typeof module !== 'undefined') module.exports = [39m
  [38;5;153m...[39m) so the browser gets a global and Node's [38;5;153mrequire[39m gets the same object. No build step.

  Node's built-in [38;5;153mnode:test[39m runner would keep the repo at zero dependencies. A linter would mean adding one (eslint +
  prettier, or biome).
